window.ANTH_INDEX=[{"k": "books", "h": "books/the-practice-of-the-presence-of-god-lawrence.html", "t": "The Practice of the Presence of God: The Best Rule of a Holy Life", "b": "Brother Lawrence", "x": "About the book Brother Lawrence (Nicolas Herman, c. 1614-1691) was a Carmelite lay brother in Paris who spent most of his life working in the monastery kitchen, and later mending sandals. He didn't write this book. After his death Joseph de Beaufort, vicar general of the archbishop of Paris, put together four conversations he had had with him in 1666-1667, his letters and a few short maxims. Highlights The method p. 13, loc. 164: \"That we need only to recognize God intimately present with us, to address ourselves to Him every moment, that we may beg His assistance for knowing His will in things doubtful, and for rightly performing those which we plainly see He requires of us, offering them to Him before we do them, and giving Him thanks when we have done.\" p. 7, loc. 82: \"That we ought to act with God in the greatest simplicity, speaking to Him frankly and plainly, and imploring His assistance in our affairs, just as they happen.\" p. 12, loc. 152: \"That there needed neither art nor science for going to God, but only a heart resolutely determined to apply itself to nothing but Him, or for His sake, and to love Him only.\" p. 34, loc. 426: \"It is not necessary for being with God to be always at church; we may make an oratory of our heart, wherein to retire from time to time, to converse with Him in meekness, humility, and love.\" Work and set times of prayer p. 14, loc. 178: \"That the most excellent method he had found of going to God, was that of doing our common business without any view of pleasing men, and (as far as we are capable) purely for the love of God.\" p. 14, loc. 183: \"That his prayer was nothing else but a sense of the presence of God, his soul being at that time insensible to everything but Divine love: and that when the appointed times of prayer were past, he found no difference, because he still continued with God.\" p. 36, loc. 447: \"One way to recollect the mind easily in the time of prayer, and preserve it more in tranquillity, is not to let it wander too far at other times.\" Wandering and coming back p. 36, loc. 444: \"If it sometimes wander, and withdraw itself from Him, do not much disquiet yourself for that; trouble and disquiet serve rather to distract the mind, than to recollect it; the will must bring it back in tranquillity.\" p. 17, loc. 221: \"When he had finished, he examined himself how he had discharged his duty; if he found well, he returned thanks to God; if otherwise, he asked pardon; and without being discouraged, he set his mind right again, and continued his exercise of the presence of God, as if he had never deviated from it.\" How the habit grows p. 7, loc. 73: \"That in order to form a habit of conversing with God continually, and referring all we do to Him; we must at first apply to Him with some diligence: but that after a little care we should find His love inwardly excite us to it without any difficulty.\" p. 21, loc. 276: \"For the first ten years I suffered much: the apprehension that I was not devoted to God,"}];